Lamlet patterns, verified: this file is also tests/fixtures/skill-patterns.lam (a symlink), so `cargo test`
checks every `expect:` line below against the interpreter. Each expect line gives the next expression region's
value. Where examples.lam shows each feature once, this file solves the problems that need several of them
together, the ones that are easy to get wrong.

$[Import* std]

1. Sorting. std has no sort; a polymorphic insertion sort is two small definitions. `Ord` comes from `<`, so the
same function sorts Ints, Floats, and Strings. Sorting by a key passes a function.
$[# {insert x sorted} {caseList sorted {^ {} {' x}} {^ {y ys} {!if {< x y} {cons x sorted} {cons y {insert x ys}}}}}]
$[# {sort xs} {fold xs {'} {^ {acc x} {insert x acc}}}]
$[# {insertBy key x sorted} {caseList sorted {^ {} {' x}} {^ {y ys} {!if {< {key x} {key y}} {cons x sorted} {cons y {insertBy key x ys}}}}}]
$[# {sortBy xs key} {fold xs {'} {^ {acc x} {insertBy key x acc}}}]
${sort {' 3 1 2}} ${sort {' "pear" "fig" "apple"}} ${map {sortBy {' {. n "b" age 3} {. n "a" age 1}} .age} .n}
expect: [1, 2, 3]
expect: ["apple", "fig", "pear"]
expect: ["a", "b"]

2. Lookup tables are lists of pairs, read through an option. Build a fixed table at compile time with `[% ...]`, and
check facts about it with `!assert`, so a wrong table fails the check instead of a later run.
$[# {lookup table key} {caseList {filter table {^ {p} {= {.first p} key}}} {^ {} none} {^ {p rest} {some {.second p}}}}]
$[# sizes [% {' {pair "s" 1} {pair "m" 2} {pair "l" 3}}]]
${withDefault {lookup sizes "m"} 0} ${withDefault {lookup sizes "xl"} 0} ${!assert {length sizes} 3}
expect: 2
expect: 0
expect: 3

3. Trees. List is the only recursive type, so a tree is an arena: a list of nodes that name their children by
index. A recursive function walks it by index (non-tail recursion is fine at this depth).
$[# tree [% {' {. label "root" kids {' 1 2}} {. label "a" kids {' 3}} {. label "b" kids {'}} {. label "c" kids {'}}}]]
$[# {count nodes i} {fold {.kids {nth nodes i}} 1 {^ {acc k} {+ acc {count nodes k}}}}]
$[# {paths nodes i prefix} [# here [f "$prefix/${.label {nth nodes i}}"]]
  {cons here {flatten {map {.kids {nth nodes i}} {^ {k} {paths nodes k here}}}}}]
${count tree 0} ${paths tree 0 ""}
expect: 4
expect: ["/root", "/root/a", "/root/a/c", "/root/b"]

4. No mutual recursion: one named function dispatches on a variant tag instead. `even`/`odd` become one function
whose argument says which question is asked.
$[# {parity q} {!case q {even n {!if {= n 0} true {parity {#odd {- n 1}}}}} {odd n {!if {= n 0} false {parity {#even {- n 1}}}}}}]
${parity {#even 10}} ${parity {#odd 7}}
expect: true
expect: true

5. Polymorphism comes from bindings, not parameters. A let-bound function can be used at two types; the same
function passed as a parameter is monomorphic inside the lambda (no higher-rank types), so pass two functions or
bind it outside.
$[# {twice f x} {f {f x}}]
$[# {both f g} {pair {twice f 1} {twice g "a"}}]
${both {^ {n} {+ n 1}} {^ {s} {++ s "!"}}}
expect: {first: 3, second: "a!!"}

6. Records are row-polymorphic in functions, but every list has one element type. To treat records of different
shapes alike, map each one to a common record first.
$[# {label r} [f "${.name r} (${.age r})"]]
$[# cat {. name "Tom" age 3 lives 9}]
$[# owner {. name "Ann" age 40 email "a@b.c"}]
${' {label cat} {label owner}}
expect: ["Tom (3)", "Ann (40)"]

7. Numbers as text. `str` of a Float always shows a fraction or an exponent; `fixed` gives a set number of places;
Int division is `div`, Float division is `/`.
${str 2.0} ${str 0.000012} ${fixed {/ 2.0 3.0} 2} ${div 7 2} ${% -7 2} ${str {round 2.5}}
expect: "2.0"
expect: "1.2e-5"
expect: "0.67"
expect: 3
expect: 1
expect: "3"

8. String helpers are folds over `chars`. A String has no indexing primitive: use `chars`, `substr`, `split`.
$[# {repeat s n} {concat {map {range 0 n} {^ {i} s}}}]
$[# {padLeft s width} {++ {repeat " " {max 0 {- width {length {chars s}}}}} s}]
$[# {startsWith s prefix} {= {substr s 0 {length {chars prefix}}} prefix}]
[${padLeft "7" 3}] ${repeat "ab" 3} ${startsWith "lamlet" "lam"} ${join "-" {reverse {split "a.b.c" "."}}}
expect: "  7"
expect: "ababab"
expect: true
expect: "c-b-a"

9. Long loops use an accumulator and a tail call; the result is built in reverse and reversed once.
$[# {squaresUpTo n} {{^ loop {i acc} {!if {> i n} {reverse acc} {loop {+ i 1} {cons {* i i} acc}}}} 1 {'}}]
${length {squaresUpTo 10000}} ${take {squaresUpTo 10000} 4}
expect: 10000
expect: [1, 4, 9, 16]

10. Errors abort everything, so "may fail" is an option (or a variant with a reason) that the caller handles.
$[# {parseDigit c} {fold {range 0 10} none {^ {found d} {!if {= {str d} c} {some d} found}}}]
$[# {parseNat s} {fold {chars s} {some 0} {^ {acc c} {caseOption acc {^ {} none} {^ {n} {caseOption {parseDigit c} {^ {} none} {^ {d} {some {+ {* n 10} d}}}}}}}}]
${parseNat "2026"} ${parseNat "20x6"}
expect: #some(2026)
expect: #none

11. State machines: a variant for the state, a variant for the event, and one pure `step`; `fold` replays a log.
$[# {step state event} {!case state
  {idle u {!case event {start n {#running n}} {tick u state} {stop u state}}}
  {running n {!case event {start m state} {tick u {#running {+ n 1}}} {stop u {#done n}}}}
  {done n state}}]
${fold {' {#start 0} {#tick unit} {#tick unit} {#stop unit} {#tick unit}} {#idle unit} step}
expect: #done(2)

12. Macros for syntax only. `!pipe` threads a value through calls, `!let` names a value, and a splice group
`{... a b}` repeats two elements together (here, a record from key-value pairs).
$[Macro !pipe {{x} x} {{x {f args ...} rest ...} {!pipe {f x args ...} rest ...}}]
$[Macro !let {{name value body} {{^ {name} body} value}}]
$[Macro !rec {{{k v} ...} {. {... k v} ...}}]
${!pipe {range 0 10} {filter {^ {n} {= {% n 2} 0}}} {map {^ {n} {* n n}}} {fold 0 +}} ${!let x 5 {* x x}} ${!rec {a 1} {b "two"}}
expect: 120
expect: 25
expect: {a: 1, b: "two"}

13. Types computed from data, at compile time, then used to check a value. A named type must be a static binding.
$[# fields [% {' "x" "y" "z"}]]
$[# Vec3 [% {Struct {map fields {^ {n} {Field n Float}}}}]]
$Vec3 ${.y {: {. x 1.0 y 2.0 z 3.0} Vec3}}
expect: {x: Float, y: Float, z: Float}
expect: 2.0

14. Curves are functions of time; combinators build new ones. A host samples them (see `lamlet-embedding`).
$[# {ease t} {* t {* t {- 3.0 {* 2.0 t}}}}]
$[# {seq a b split} {^ {t} {!if {< t split} {a {/ t split}} {b {/ {- t split} {- 1.0 split}}}}}]
$[# {between from to} {^ {t} {lerp from to {ease {clamp t 0.0 1.0}}}}]
$[# path {seq {between 0.0 10.0} {between 10.0 0.0} 0.5}]
${map {' 0.0 0.25 0.5 0.75 1.0} path}
expect: [0.0, 5.0, 10.0, 5.0, 0.0]
